BCC_Q({"lv": "910", "y": 2024, "n": 10, "part": "B", "topic": "graph", "title": "Railway Network", "answer": 0, "story": "<p>In the land of Bebravia, there are six towns. All food is grown in\nQuandaria and transported by train to the other towns. The train routes\nare indicated by lines drawn between towns in the diagram. The arrow on\neach line indicates the direction in which trains carrying food are\nallowed to move.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"A description of the\ndiagram follows.\" src=\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\" style=\"width:550px\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdesc2')\" style=\"box-shadow: none;\">Hide/Reveal Description of Diagram for Railway Network<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdesc2\" style=\"display: none;\">\n<p>Four unnamed towns arranged to form the vertices of a square (top\nleft, top right, bottom left, bottom right) with the town Quandria to\nthe left of the square and the town Pixelton to the right. Labelled\narrows point between the towns as follows:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>From Quandria: An arrow labelled 8 points to the top left town\nand and arrow labelled 11 points to the bottom left town.<\/p><\/li>\n<li><p>From the top left town: An arrow labelled 2 points to the top\nright town and an arrow labelled 3 points to the bottom left\ntown.<\/p><\/li>\n<li><p>From the bottom left town: An arrow labelled 7 points to the top\nright town and an arrow labelled 4 points to the bottom right\ntown.<\/p><\/li>\n<li><p>From the top right town: An arrow labelled 9 point to the bottom\nright town and an arrow labelled 12 points to Pixleton.<\/p><\/li>\n<li><p>From the bottom right town: An arrow labelled 10 points to\nPixleton.<\/p><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/><\/div>\n<p>Bebravia is not large so there is more than enough time for many\ntrains to travel between any two towns. The only limitation is the daily\ncapacity on each train route: the number on each line indicates the\nmaximum number of trains that can travel along that route on one\nday.<\/p>", "question": "<p>In one day, what is the largest number of trains that can travel from\nQuandaria to Pixleton?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li>13<\/li>\n<li>16<\/li>\n<li>19<\/li>\n<li>12<\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(A) 13<\/p>", "explanation": "<p>We consider different routes from Quandria to Pixleton. One path uses\nlines along the top of the diagram and it can accommodate up to two\ntrains.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"A path goes from Quandria\nto the top left town, using 2 out of 8 slots, then from the top left town to the top right town, using 2 out of 2 slots, then from the top right town to Pixleton, using 2 out of 12 slots.\" src=\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\" style=\"width:550px\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>A completely separate second path uses lines along the bottom of the\ndiagram and it can accommodate up to four trains.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"A path goes from Quandria\nto the bottom left town, using 4 out of 11 slots, then from the bottom left town to the bottom right town, using 4 out of 4 slots, then from the bottom right town to Pixleton, using 4 out of 10 slots.\" src=\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\" style=\"width:550px\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>A third path that can accommodate 7 trains is shown below.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"A path goes from Quandria\nto the bottom left town, using 4 plus 7 out of 11 slots, then from the bottom left town to the top right town, using 7 out of 7 slots, then from the top right town to Pixleton, using 2 plus 7 out of 12 slots.\" src=\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\" style=\"width:550px\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>Note that even though this third path overlaps with the two previous\npaths, there is still capacity for all three paths to be fully used\nmeaning a total of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2+4+7=13\\)<\/span> trains\ncan use these paths to travel from Quandria to Pixleton.<\/p>\n<p>We can determine that 13 is the largest number of trains that can\ntravel from Quandria to Pixleton by looking at the three lines in the\nmiddle of the diagram with capacities 2, 7 and 4. All paths from\nQuandria to Pixleton use one of these lines, so it is not possible for\nmore than <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2+7+4=13\\)<\/span> trains to go\nfrom Quandria to Pixleton.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>This problem is an instance of a <em>maximum flow problem<\/em>.\nMaximum flow problems involve determining the maximum amount of\n<em>flow<\/em> from a <em>source<\/em> node to a <em>sink<\/em> node in a\n<em>graph<\/em>, where each <em>edge<\/em> has a maximum capacity.<\/p>\n<p>Maximum flow problems can be solved using various algorithms: three\nwell-known algorithms are the <em>Ford-Fulkerson<\/em> algorithm, the\n<em>Edmonds-Karp<\/em> algorithm, and <em>Dinic’s<\/em> algorithm. Each of\nthese algorithms has advantages and disadvantages in terms of runtime\nefficiency, ease of implementation, and applicability to different types\nof networks. The choice of algorithm depends on factors such as the size\nof the network, the nature of the capacities, and the desired level of\noptimization.<\/p>\n<p>The maximum flow is directly related to the <em>minimum cut<\/em>: the\nleast amount of capacity on edges which could be removed entirely that\nseparates the source node from the sink node. As pointed out in the\nExplanation of Answer, the minimum cut in this task has capacity 13, and\nif these edges were removed, there would be no way to travel from the\nsource node (Quandaria) to the sink node (Pixleton).<\/p>\n<p>Maximum flow algorithms have many applications: traffic optimization,\ndistribution systems (e.g., water and electricity), resource allocation,\nand computer networking, to name a few.<\/p>"});